# FR-87: generated from git history by `bun docs/dates.ts --write` just before
# an image build (hosted-image.yml). A committed copy would go stale at once.
ui/docs/content-dates.json
/models/
media/
ui/test-results/
ui/playwright-report/
ui/e2e/video/output/
# Everything under .claude/ is local, EXCEPT the hostname-sanitisation tooling,
# which has to be TRACKED. Note the shape: git never descends into an excluded
# DIRECTORY to look for a negation inside it, so a plain `.claude/` followed by
# a `!` deeper down does nothing. The exclusion has to be re-stated with `/*` at
# each level so the one path being kept stays reachable.
#
# This exists because the old `.claude/` rule silently ate this tool once
# already: commit 2e68cf3 (2026-08-28) is titled "...+ add the sweep as a
# skill", and its diffstat is a balanced 97/97 rename with no new files -- the
# skill it claimed to add never entered the repo at all, so it was gone by the
# next session and the sweep had to be rebuilt from scratch. A prevention
# mechanism that is not committed is not a prevention mechanism.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/
.claude/skills/*
!.claude/skills/sanitize-hostnames/
.claude/skills/sanitize-hostnames/__pycache__/
# encoder-cells and ship-it carry the invariants that used to sit in CLAUDE.md,
# loaded on demand instead of on every prompt. They name no host and no IP, so
# unlike the four fleet skills they belong in the public repo -- and a rule a
# contributor's agent never loads is not a rule.
!.claude/skills/encoder-cells/
!.claude/skills/ship-it/
# Same reasoning one directory over: .claude/hooks/ holds the GitHub-write
# identity guard and its selftest, which CI RUNS. Ignored, they would not be in
# the repo at all -- the CI job would fail on a missing file, and the guard
# would exist only on the machine that wrote it. That is exactly how the
# sanitisation skill above was lost once already.
!.claude/hooks/
# The FR-backlog autopilot: the orchestration skill and the worker agent it
# dispatches. Same argument a third time -- an orchestrator that lives only on
# the machine that wrote it is one session away from being rebuilt from scratch.
!.claude/skills/autopilot/
!.claude/agents/
# ...and settings.json, which wires the hook above and pins the effort level.
# `settings.local.json` stays out: it is one machine's absolute paths.
!.claude/settings.json
#
# ⚠️ The REST of .claude/skills/ stays ignored ON PURPOSE, and this is a
# security boundary rather than tidiness: `vmtest`, `profiletest`,
# `whitelist-ip` and `tunnel-fleet-test` carry live fleet addresses and
# `ssh user@host` lines (13 occurrences, measured 2026-09-08). This repository
# is PUBLIC, and a git history is not something you can take back -- the
# identity rewrite of 2026-09-06 proved that, leaving 529 commits reachable
# through refs/pull/* forever. Those skills belong sanitised, or in the private
# meshharbor-docs repo. Do not "finish the job" by deleting this rule.
dist/
# The image's GeoIP country database and its provenance note, staged by
# scripts/fetch-geoip.sh before `docker build` (#1896): fetched, never
# committed. Everything but the README, so a GeoLite2 file someone drops in
# stays out of git too.
files/geoip/*
!files/geoip/README.md

# Code-signing operator state + local key material (scripts/signing/).
# The state file holds only public identifiers, but dev-cert/ holds a real
# private key -- never commit either.
scripts/signing/.meshharbor-signing.json
scripts/signing/dev-cert/
scripts/signing/apple/
# gpg/: everything is secret EXCEPT the public key, which must be committed
# so the API can serve it (include_str! in setup_release.rs).
scripts/signing/gpg/*
!scripts/signing/gpg/meshharbor-release-pubkey.asc
*.pfx
*.p12
*.key
*.p8

# Tauri writes one ACL schema per HOST platform into `gen/schemas/`. The repo
# tracks the ones the release lanes actually build (desktop / windows); a
# `linux-schema.json` only appears when someone compiles the companion on
# Linux — as the WSL backend lane does — and committing it would put a local
# build artifact in every such session's diff. FR-27.
agents/*/gen/schemas/linux-schema.json

# Session handover notes, written to the repo root by convention and never
# meant to be published. This repo is PUBLIC, and an untracked file here is
# one `git add -A` away from being in it — which already nearly happened with
# `apple-authority-letter.md` (a signed company document naming a private
# address and phone number), caught only because someone looked. Three more
# were sitting in the same position when this rule landed. The pattern is the
# guard, not anyone's diligence.
HANDOVER-*.md
